// @test: run
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: ffi, name-resolution, externs
// @description: A function whose name happens to be a libc symbol is an
//   ordinary function.
//
// It was not. The guard that stops an extern's own call from being
// stolen by another module's same-named wrapper tested "the winner
// arrived under a QUALIFIED name" — and every user function is
// registered as `<module>.<name>`, so this module's own function
// counted as foreign. The guard switched itself off and the call was
// emitted as the POSIX symbol:
//
//     fn read(x: &Boxed) -> Int { x.a }
//     read(&v)
//     error: FFI runtime error: argument count mismatch: expected 3, got 1
//
// naming an extern the author never wrote. Renaming the function to
// anything outside libc fixed it, which is not a property a program
// should have to know about — `read`, `write` and `open` are ordinary
// names for ordinary functions, and no program can be asked to avoid
// every POSIX symbol.
//
// FOREIGN now means "belongs to another module" rather than "is
// qualified".
//
// THIS FILE DECLARES A MODULE, and that is load-bearing rather than
// decoration. A file with no `module X;` header has no scope to prefer
// its own declarations by, and there the call is bound to the standard
// library's `read` before the FFI decision is ever reached — a
// different defect, in name resolution rather than in FFI emission,
// filed separately. Writing the header here keeps this spec measuring
// one thing.
//
// This file declares NO externs, which is the case under test. The
// sibling `extern_beats_foreign_namesake.vr` covers the opposite
// polarity — a file that DOES declare an extern must reach it — and the
// two must both hold, since a fix for either alone breaks the other.
//
// @expected-stdout-begin
// read=7
// write=7
// open=x
// close=1
// @expected-stdout-end

module libc_named;

public type Boxed is { a: Int };

/// Every name here is a libc symbol and none of them is an extern.
fn read(x: &Boxed) -> Int {
    x.a
}

fn write(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
    a + b
}

fn open(p: Text) -> Text {
    p.clone()
}

fn close(n: Int) -> Int {
    n
}

fn main() {
    let v = Boxed { a: 7 };
    print(f"read={read(&v)}");
    print(f"write={write(3, 4)}");
    print(f"open={open("x")}");
    print(f"close={close(1)}");
}
